/* book Worked example: Q: A rectangular pulse of amplitude A = 1 V and duration T = 1 ms is received in white noise with one-sided density N₀ = 2 × 10⁻⁶ W/Hz (take the voltage across 1 Ω). Find (a) the pulse energy, (b) the matched-filter output at t = T, (c) the maximum output SNR as a ratio and in dB.
   1. E = A²T = 1² × 1 × 10⁻³ = 1 × 10⁻³ J = 1 mJ. (V²·s across 1 Ω is W·s, that is J.)
   2. The matched filter is h(t) = s(T − t), a 1 V rectangle on 0 ≤ t ≤ 1 ms. Its signal output at T equals E = 1 × 10⁻³ (in V²·s).
   3. Noise variance at the output: σ² = (N₀/2)E = 1 × 10⁻⁶ × 1 × 10⁻³ = 1 × 10⁻⁹.
   4. SNR = E²/σ² = (10⁻³)²/10⁻⁹ = 1000. Check: 2E/N₀ = 2 × 10⁻³/(2 × 10⁻⁶) = 1000.
   5. In dB: 10 log₁₀ 1000 = 30 dB.
   Answer: E = 1 mJ, output at T is 1 × 10⁻³ V²·s, maximum SNR = 1000 = 30 dB. No other filter does better at t = T. */
