/* book Worked example 2: Q: For the same pulse and noise: (a) the sampler fires at 0.8T instead of T; (b) a different pulse, the ramp s(t) = At/T on 0 ≤ t ≤ T, is received with the same rectangular filter (integrate and dump) instead of its own matched filter. Find the SNR loss in each case.
   1. (a) The matched-filter output for the rectangle is the triangle y(t) = A²t for 0 ≤ t ≤ T, so y(0.8T) = 0.8E. The noise variance is unchanged at (N₀/2)E.
   2. SNR(0.8T) = (0.8E)²/((N₀/2)E) = 0.64 × 2E/N₀ = 0.64 × 1000 = 640, a loss of 10 log₁₀ 0.64 = −1.94 dB (28.06 dB instead of 30 dB).
   3. (b) Ramp energy E = ∫₀ᵀ (At/T)² dt = A²T/3. Integrate-and-dump output: ∫₀ᵀ (At/T) dt = AT/2; its noise variance is (N₀/2)T.
   4. SNR = (AT/2)²/((N₀/2)T) = A²T/(2N₀). The matched filter would give 2E/N₀ = 2A²T/(3N₀).
   5. Ratio = [A²T/(2N₀)]/[2A²T/(3N₀)] = 3/4 = 0.75, a loss of 10 log₁₀ 0.75 = −1.25 dB. This is ρ² for a ramp against a flat reference: (1/2)²/(1/3) = 0.75.
   Answer: (a) Sampling at 0.8T costs 1.94 dB (SNR 640); (b) the flat filter on a ramp pulse costs 1.25 dB (factor 0.75). */
