/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A bank of N correlators, one per basis function, turns r(t) into a vector r = sm + w when signal m was sent.
   - The maximum-likelihood (ML) rule chooses the m for which r is most likely.
   - m̂ = the m with the smallest ‖r − sm‖
   - Here this is the minimum-distance rule.
   - Expanding the distance, ‖r − sm‖² = ‖r‖² − 2⟨r, sm⟩ + Em.
   - The decision region of sm is the set of all r that are decided as m.
   - Q(x) = (1/√(2π)) ∫ₓ^∞ e−u²/2 du
   - Here Q(x) is the probability that a zero-mean, unit-variance Gaussian exceeds x.
   - For two points a distance d apart, an error happens when the noise component along the line joining them exceeds d/2.
   - Pe = Q( (d/2)/√(N₀/2) ) = Q( d/√(2N₀) )
*/
