/* book Worked example: Q: Polar binary signalling is used at Eb/N₀ = 10 dB. (a) Find the bit error probability. (b) Find the Eb/N₀ that gives Pe = 10⁻⁵. (c) What would on-off signalling give at 10 dB?
   1. Convert from dB first: Eb/N₀ = 1010/10 = 10.
   2. (a) x = √(2 × 10) = √20 = 4.472. Pe = Q(4.472) = ½ erfc(4.472/√2) = ½ erfc(3.162) = 3.87 × 10⁻⁶. It must lie between Q(4.5) = 3.40 × 10⁻⁶ and Q(4) = 3.17 × 10⁻⁵, and it does.
   3. (b) Solve Q(x) = 10⁻⁵: x = 4.265. Then 2Eb/N₀ = x² = 18.19, so Eb/N₀ = 9.09, that is 10 log₁₀ 9.09 = 9.59 dB.
   4. (c) On-off: Pe = Q(√10) = Q(3.162) = 7.83 × 10⁻⁴, about 200 times worse at the same Eb/N₀.
   Answer: (a) 3.87 × 10⁻⁶; (b) 9.59 dB; (c) 7.83 × 10⁻⁴. */
