/* book Worked example 2: Q: Use the three points of the Gram-Schmidt example, s1 = (1, 0), s2 = (1, 1), s3 = (1, −1) V·√ms, equally likely, with N₀ = 0.05 V²·ms. (a) Decide r = (1.4, 0.3) by the minimum-distance rule and by the correlation metric, and show what goes wrong if Em/2 is dropped. (b) Find the average symbol error probability.
   1. (a) Distances: ‖r − s1‖ = √(0.4² + 0.3²) = 0.500; ‖r − s2‖ = √(0.4² + 0.7²) = 0.806; ‖r − s3‖ = √(0.4² + 1.3²) = 1.360. Nearest is s1.
   2. Correlations ⟨r, sm⟩: 1.4, 1.4 + 0.3 = 1.7, 1.4 − 0.3 = 1.1. Energies 1, 2, 2. Metrics ⟨r, sm⟩ − Em/2: 0.9, 0.7, 0.1. Largest is s1, agreeing with the distances.
   3. Dropping Em/2 would pick the largest correlation, 1.7, that is s2: wrong, because the energies are not equal.
   4. (b) The boundaries are the horizontal lines φ2 = +0.5 and φ2 = −0.5, each 0.5 from its neighbours. The noise along φ2 has σ = √(N₀/2) = √0.025 = 0.158.
   5. One boundary crossing: Q(0.5/0.158) = Q(3.162) = 7.83 × 10⁻⁴. s2 and s3 have one boundary each; s1 has two, so its error is 2 × 7.83 × 10⁻⁴.
   6. Average: Pe = (2 + 1 + 1)/3 × 7.83 × 10⁻⁴ = 1.04 × 10⁻³.
   Answer: (a) r is decided as s1 (distance 0.500, metric 0.9); the bare correlation would wrongly pick s2. (b) Pe = 1.04 × 10⁻³. */
